en.description:
The tomb was built for Sinan Yusuf Paşa, who was the Governor of Karaman during the last years of the Ottoman Sultan Selim II, when Mimar Sinan was the chief architect. The tomb, located behind the Fatma Hatun Tomb, is made of Gödene stone with an octagonal plan and a flat dome. The following is written on the inscription on the door;

which is translated as: “Sinan Paşa virüb cân-ı ‘azîzî Olüp kûy-i şehîdâne revâne, O Yûsuf-sîyrete ahsen-i history Sinan Paşa mahal Mısr-i cinâne 981.”

The flat-domed tomb, which is one of the classical Ottoman period tomb styles, has an octagonal body and is made of Gödene stone. The tomb, which is on a bench high above the ground, is entered through a double-winged wooden door with round arches and marble jambs made of black and white stones in alternating order. The tomb has seven skylights, three rectangular windows at the bottom, three circles above the rectangular windows at the top, and four pointed arches on the surfaces where the niches are located. The windows are covered with iron grids. The floor where the sky marble sarcophagus is located in the middle of the tomb is paved with stone.
